A **dot plot** places one dot for each observation, stacked above its value on a number line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (-0.6,0)--(6.4,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (\x,0.1)--(\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x/\c in {0/1,1/3,2/4,3/2,5/1} {\foreach \k in {1,...,\c} {\fill (\x,{0.35*\k}) circle (2.2pt);}}
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.9) {Value};
\node at (2.5,2.3) {Dot plot};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **stem-and-leaf plot** splits each value into a **stem** (all but the last digit) and a **leaf** (the last digit), and needs a **key**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\node at (0,1.6) {Stem};
\node at (1.7,1.6) {Leaf};
\draw (0.75,1.3)--(0.75,-0.7);
\draw (-0.6,1.3)--(3.0,1.3);
\node at (0.35,0.9) {$1$};
\node at (0.35,0.3) {$2$};
\node at (0.35,-0.3) {$3$};
\node[anchor=west] at (1.0,0.9) {$2\;\; 5\;\; 8$};
\node[anchor=west] at (1.0,0.3) {$0\;\; 3\;\; 3\;\; 7$};
\node[anchor=west] at (1.0,-0.3) {$1\;\; 6$};
\node[anchor=west,font=\small] at (-0.6,-1.3) {Key: $2 \mid 3$ means $23$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **column graph** draws an equal-width bar for each category, with gaps between the bars.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(7.6,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.6,0) rectangle (1.8,3);
\draw (2.2,0) rectangle (3.4,6);
\draw (3.8,0) rectangle (5.0,4);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.6,5);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,0) {$A$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.8,0) {$B$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.4,0) {$C$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.0,0) {$D$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,3.5) {Frequency};
\node[below] at (3.6,-1.0) {Category};
\node at (3.6,7.1) {Column graph};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **frequency histogram** draws the same bars for numerical scores but with **no gaps**, each column centred on its score.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(7.6,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.9,0) rectangle (2.3,3);
\draw (2.3,0) rectangle (3.7,6);
\draw (3.7,0) rectangle (5.1,4);
\draw (5.1,0) rectangle (6.5,5);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.6,0) {$1$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.0,0) {$2$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.4,0) {$3$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.8,0) {$4$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,3.5) {Frequency};
\node[below] at (3.7,-1.0) {Score};
\node at (3.7,7.1) {Frequency histogram};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

